    "Nino (c. 296–338/340) was a Cappadocian Christian woman, traditionally said to be a relative of St. George, who came to the Kingdom of Iberia (eastern Georgia) around 320 AD, reportedly carrying a cross made of grapevine bound with her own hair.",
    "Through her preaching, healing miracles, and the eventual conversion of Queen Nana and King Mirian III after he was saved from a sudden darkness while hunting, Nino brought about the conversion of the Georgian royal court and, through it, the whole kingdom to Christianity, making Georgia one of the earliest Christian nations.",
    "She spent her final years evangelizing in the Georgian mountains and is buried at Bodbe, where a monastery was built in her honor. Nino is venerated as 'Equal-to-the-Apostles' and is one of the most beloved saints of the Georgian Orthodox Church, with her feast celebrated January 14 and the Grapevine Cross remaining a central Georgian Christian symbol."
